Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 21:45:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09312/11362] Correct the induce-timeout entry: I divided a
 VRAM cap and called it a risk

I quoted main.py:589-590 without reading the comment at :583-588 directly above them. It
says max_tokens is a CAP whose unused headroom costs pool VRAM and never wall clock, and
it records the number my entry claimed was unavailable: the worst measured Qwen3.8
induction is ~1053s against a 2400s timeout, 2.3x headroom, set deliberately clear of the
measured worst case. Dividing the cap by the timeout is not a risk computation.

The codebase had also already done that exact division and explained it away -- :686-688
records kernel v27's banner doing 131072-over-900s, diagnosed as arithmetic rather than a
fault, after which the probe was rebuilt to stop generating to the cap. Filing the ratio
would have re-raised an alarm this repo already caught and designed out. repair-extend-2
caught it before it reached the operator.

The finding that survives uses the observed 1053s length instead of the ceiling: converted
to ~55k tokens and re-timed at measured per-stream rates, it fits at k=1, 4, 16 and at the
shipped k=24 default with 9% margin, and exceeds 2400s at k=32. Driven by a length actually
observed, so it survives the cap-is-not-a-target objection.

Original entry left in place per never-prune with the correction appended above its
cross-references.

